"go: do it. ask: it reaches outside what the department makes (money, a message to someone, a change to the goal), so the owner stamps first. refuse: it breaks a rule. Reading the web is not outside: the department can read any page.\nunsure: facts the request says the owner is not sure of, in a few words each; [] when none.\nrule: a standing rule the request states (always or never, from now on) with its tag; null when it is one piece of work."
"tag goal: the directive changes what the department is for. scope one-time: the owner means this once."
"A correction names the item it puts right and the finding it answers. Name the findings it fixes (fixes) and the items it covers (covers), each from the lists above. Never repeat the approach that already failed: when the last round failed, say a different thing, and do not ask for more of the same. State no fact nobody gave: where a fact is missing, say it is to be confirmed."
"An engine you keep reads what an earlier one wrote, so leave out only an engine that no engine you keep reads. A note is one line about this goal, at most 300 characters, and says what the engine does differently; give an engine no note when it does what it always does. Name only engines of the line above. When nothing needs to change, answer with the whole line and an empty fit."
"For each engine give its name and what it does, the items it covers (covers), its level (1 or 2), how sure you are (confidence, a number from 0 to 1) and, when you are below 0.8 sure, what you doubt (doubt).\nLevel 1 takes an engine of the Library as it is, with the reads and writes shown: use it when its use case fits the goal. Level 2 is a new engine: name it in one or two words none of the taken names use, and name what it writes in one or two words. Keep every engine this kind of department requires, at level 1.\nConfidence is how sure you are that this engine, reading what the plan hands it, can make its items true without a fact or a decision nobody has given. In doubt write the one question you would ask the owner, in one sentence; you may add a second sentence that begins \"Unless you say,\" and says what you will do if they do not answer.\nAn engine can search the web and open pages: say in what it does what it looks up, and mark internet true.\nAnswer cannot, with no engines, only when an engine this kind of department requires cannot serve an item: name the engine, the items and why."
"high: a reader could be harmed or misled by it. At most eight findings."
"One row for every item, each under its own id, and no other row. A finding is something to put right that you saw while reading: say where it is and what is wrong, and name the item it belongs to, or null. high: a reader could be harmed or misled by it. Never guess a fact that neither the brief nor a filed artifact gives: a phone number, an address, a date, a count, a name. A fact a filed artifact gives, with its source, is backed: do not report it. At most eight findings."
"At most four findings."
